  "reveal": {
    "title": "Hagia Sophia, Istanbul",
    "summary": "Hagia Sophia is the great domed monument of Istanbul, Turkey. Commissioned by Justinian I as a Christian church in the sixth century, it later served as a mosque, museum, and mosque again. Its architecture and layered decoration embody the city's Byzantine and Ottoman histories.",
    "clueExplanation": "The strait, Justinianic origin, central dome, conversion after the fifteenth-century conquest, minarets, and layered mosaics all identify Hagia Sophia. UNESCO's Historic Areas record places it among the monuments of Istanbul's historic peninsula, where the former imperial capital's major sites stand close together.",
    "sourceIds": ["britannica-hagia", "unesco-historic-areas"]
  },
